import{_ as t,o,c as s,a0 as a}from"./chunks/framework.ChlVJx0I.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"The LSP client is in-process, on the official protocol packages","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0041-the-lsp-client-is-in-process-on-the-official-protocol-packages.md","filePath":"adr/0041-the-lsp-client-is-in-process-on-the-official-protocol-packages.md","lastUpdated":1792093740000}'),n={name:"adr/0041-the-lsp-client-is-in-process-on-the-official-protocol-packages.md"};function i(r,e,c,p,l,h){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="the-lsp-client-is-in-process-on-the-official-protocol-packages" tabindex="-1">The LSP client is in-process, on the official protocol packages <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-lsp-client-is-in-process-on-the-official-protocol-packages" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The LSP client is in-process, on the official protocol packages&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Issue #136 needs a client that speaks the Language Server Protocol to a server process for the lifetime of a pi session, and that can attach results to pi&#39;s own tool results. Three shapes were on the table.</p><p><strong>The extension speaks LSP itself, in the pi process, on <code>vscode-jsonrpc</code> plus <code>vscode-languageserver-protocol</code>.</strong> The two packages are the protocol&#39;s own definition: framing, request and notification plumbing, and the typed message shapes. They are pure JavaScript with no native code, which matters in a package that already carries one heavy native dependency (codegraph). The client lives in the session process, so the <code>tool_result</code> hook, the Sweep, the settings, and the server lifecycle all sit in one place.</p><p><strong>Considered options.</strong> Registering an existing MCP LSP bridge through <code>pi.registerMcpServer()</code> is the obvious shortcut, and it is wrong here for three reasons: those bridges expose 50 to 65 tools, which is the context cost this package spends real effort avoiding; the Post-edit annotation needs a <code>tool_result</code> handler over pi&#39;s own <code>edit</code> and <code>write</code>, which an external server cannot install; and an external binary takes the Server registry, the settings, and the failure reporting out of this repo&#39;s reach. Hand-rolling JSON-RPC framing and the message types saves two dependencies and buys a permanent maintenance tax on a protocol with a maintained implementation. A daemon shared between sessions is rejected in ADR 0043.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> The protocol packages are pinned as npm dependencies of this package, so an LSP spec version bump is a dependency bump rather than a rewrite, and the extension&#39;s message handling is checked against their types. The dependency pair is small enough that swapping to a hand-rolled client later stays possible; the lock-in this ADR actually records is the decision to stay in-process, which is what makes the <code>tool_result</code> hook and the server lifecycle one design instead of two.</p>',5)])])}const f=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,f as default};
